CONCEPT
        State-preserving blueprint updates

DESCRIPTION
        update_blueprint() changes programs and variable layouts of existing
        ordinary LPC objects. It preserves object identity, retained live
        values, inventory/environment links, heartbeats, and callouts. The
        complete selected cohort is prepared before any program is installed.
        A precommit incompatibility leaves that cohort unchanged.

        This differs from destructing and reloading objects. There is no
        constructor, reset, __INIT, or migration callback during installation.
        Variable addition, removal, and reordering are supported in the first
        implementation. Matching uses declaring program, inheritance occurrence,
        and declaration name; a physical slot number is not an identity.
        Retained values follow those identities even when their slots move.
        Removed values are released only after every selected object's new
        program and variable block have been installed. External references to
        values and protected aliases retain their ordinary LPC semantics.

        Existing-variable initializers are never replayed. New independently
        initialized variables require supported captured literal/implicit
        defaults: integers, floats, strings/bytes, arrays, and mappings whose
        components are representable within the bounds. Arbitrary initializer
        code is not evaluated to obtain a default. Under blueprint sharing,
        clone additions copy the prepared/current blueprint value instead;
        reports describe that decision without exposing the live value.
        Missing metadata, unsupported expressions, incompatible RTT defaults,
        changed variable types/modifiers, incompatible callable signatures,
        changed inheritance, and incompatible struct layouts block migration.
        Function argument schema metadata is separate from saved argument types;
        there is no blanket requirement to add pragma save_types.

        Supported named native lfun/variable closures retain identity through
        migration. Supported Python Function/Variable wrappers for ordinary
        objects share those named bindings. Existing and freshly obtained
        equivalent wrappers retain hash/equality and dictionary/set behavior,
        while calls, variable access, metadata, and namespace views use the
        current program. A retained declaration that is removed or resolves
        ambiguously blocks the whole cohort. Native and Python owners are
        both checked. Unsupported generated/raw closures, unfinished coroutines,
        and Python function/variable iterators (even exhausted ones) remain
        blockers. Lightweight objects and their handles are not migrated.

        Only ordinary blueprints and same-family ordinary clones qualify.
        Interactive, shadowing, shadowed, replaced, virtual, master, primary
        and backup simul-efun objects are excluded. Pending replace_program()
        work anywhere in the family conflicts with the request. These checks
        apply again at execution; creating an unsupported dependency after
        submission can make the deferred attempt fail.

SOURCE AND SELECTION
        A string source names an already loaded blueprint. The driver pins
        that identity and its exact old program, then privately compiles the
        source file during execution against the captured inherited programs.
        The new candidate becomes public only on successful installation.
        The source blueprint always participates in this mode, including
        update_blueprint(path, ({})), which selects no clones.

        An object source uses that loaded blueprint's exact current program
        as its candidate. It does not compile another program or implicitly
        update the blueprint. This mode can migrate clones from older
        generations to the currently loaded generation. Already-current clones
        count as matched/already_current and are left unchanged; dependencies
        on their unchanged program do not block the update.

        Omitted targets and integer 0 select all matching clones discovered
        at execution after compiler/master callbacks. An array selects its
        copied, deduplicated object identities; ({}) means no clones. Passing
        the source blueprint in the array does not count it as a clone or
        duplicate its implicit participation. Destroyed explicit targets are
        skipped after admission; unrelated or unsupported surviving targets
        reject the request. Discovery counts are completed before survivor
        rejection, or explicitly reported as unavailable on a scan limit.

        clones(path, 0), clones(path, 1), and clones(path, 2) still select by
        creation/load history, not current installed program. Migration does
        not alter that history. blueprint(ob) follows ob's currently installed
        program and returns its blueprint object, if still available. Neither
        efun substitutes for the report's actual migration cohort counts.

REQUEST LIFETIME AND AUTHORITY
        There are exactly two driver efuns: update_blueprint(source[,targets])
        returns a positive ID, and update_blueprint_result(id) observes it.
        There is no public prepare, queue, commit, cancel, or discard operation.
        Invalid admission raises before an ID exists. The driver calls
        privilege_violation("update_blueprint", caller, source, targets) and
        revalidates identities and limits after that hook. The requesting
        object owns the ID; only it or the master may read the driver report.
        A wizard who initiated a mudlib command is not automatically that owner.

        Execution is one deferred attempt on the driver thread after the
        current stack unwinds, at the periodic backend boundary before
        heartbeat/callout dispatch. It is not background worker-thread work.
        Requests submitted by callbacks after the batch was detached wait for
        the following tick. Polling never advances the operation.

        Pending reports contain only zero/empty unknowns. Completed means the
        cohort was installed; failed means it was rejected before installation.
        Once installation finishes, the completed outcome is immutable, even
        if retiring old values invokes a finalizer that fails or destroys the
        owner. Compiler/master-hook external side effects are outside rollback.
        The family remains reserved until retirement completes.

        Owner destruction cancels its uncommitted work and removes its failed
        reports. Committed reports remain readable by the master. Terminal
        driver reports expire after 300 seconds, without renewal on reads;
        the cache retains at most 256, evicting oldest completion then lowest
        ID. All cleanup is automatic. A mudlib polling timeout has no effect
        on the request's driver state.

DIAGNOSTICS
        update_blueprint_result(E) defines every public field, conditional
        declaration/default shape, and error category. Reports have thirteen
        top-level fields. Nested arrays/mappings are fresh independent copies;
        mutating a result cannot change another result or the driver's cache.
        Terminal evidence contains only copied native data, never object,
        program, closure, or Python references.

        The first error preserves the primary failure. Later rows include
        compiler evidence, shared generation/schema blockers, and per-instance
        rows with exact object_name() strings and clone/blueprint roles.
        Named blockers identify declaration kind, program, name, occurrence,
        and explicit dispatch when needed. Removed-variable evidence remains
        available on completed and ordinarily failed requests. A completed
        schema pass also survives a later diagnostic allocation failure.

        Independent safe reasons are collected after ordinary incompatibility.
        Diagnostics are bounded, not an unqualified exhaustive scan after an
        invalid source or resource failure. An incomplete marker explicitly
        distinguishes limited evidence. Later byte/work limits preserve the
        primary code/message and add incomplete_code/incomplete_message.
        No omitted count is guessed. There is no 128-instance cohort cap.

LIMITS
        The feature is off by default; enable --enable-blueprint-update.
        Configurable defaults are:
          --with-blueprint-update-inflight=64
          --with-blueprint-update-owner=8
          --with-blueprint-update-targets=10000
          --with-blueprint-update-scan-objects=1000000
          --with-blueprint-update-variable-slots=1000000
          --with-blueprint-update-bytes=67108864
          --with-blueprint-update-literal-depth=64

        A canonical family may have only one in-flight request, even with
        disjoint explicit selections. Target limits count unique clones;
        raw array input and all-mode traversal have a separate scan bound.
        Variable limits include new staging and old retirement slots. The
        byte allowance covers retained preparation, report data, checked
        diagnostic scratch, and conservative bookkeeping; it is per request,
        not a global reservation. Temporary compilation and LPC values also
        obey ordinary driver limits.

        The shared schema/runtime/identity budget is 1000000 work steps.
        Schema inheritance traversal has depth bound 64 and occurrence-path
        storage of 2048 bytes. Named identity has a 4096-byte internal
        representability bound; its private encoding is not a public field.
        Literal compiler scratch and frozen evidence each use the configured
        byte bound; offsets/counts must fit 32 bits, captured literal arrays
        have at most 65535 elements, and nesting uses the configured depth.
        Exceeding optional capture bounds does not make ordinary LPC syntax
        invalid, but unavailable evidence can block a later update.

        Compiler diagnostics have caps of 128 rows and 65536 text bytes.
        Each generation/role has 128 schema blockers plus an overflow marker.
        Runtime count/reservation/fill passes and final validation share the
        byte/work budgets; weak registry traversal and commit do not allocate,
        release references, or invoke callbacks. Each result copy has fresh
        byte/work bounds and obeys normal caller array/mapping/evaluation limits.

REUSABLE COORDINATOR
        mudlib/blueprint_update.c supplies two public operations:
          int submit(mixed source [, mixed targets])
          mapping result(int id)

        Inherit it in a long-lived local coordinator and implement these
        protected hooks, all of which deny by default:
          object blueprint_update_initiator(object caller)
          int blueprint_update_allow_submit(object caller, object initiator,
                                            mixed source, mixed targets)
          int blueprint_update_allow_result(object caller, object initiator,
                                            int id)

        Resolve the wizard through trusted mudlib command/session ownership.
        The actual immediate caller is captured separately and must not be
        replaced with a user-supplied wizard or this_player() guess. The submit
        hook receives a separate copy of explicit selection evidence; mutating
        it cannot alter the submitted array. The driver's master privilege
        hook independently authorizes the coordinator, which invokes both efuns
        and therefore owns every underlying ID.

        Public result callers must be the stored caller or initiating wizard
        and pass current result policy. Internal polling passes the stored
        caller and initiator to that policy, rather than inventing a
        previous_object() context. Revocation prevents automatic report
        delivery. Notifications go to the initiating wizard with tell_object().
        The command's caller can read the returned mapping and display its
        detailed errors/variable_changes in the mudlib's normal operator UI.

        The default local bounds are 64 preallocated bookkeeping slots, one
        housekeeping callout every 2 seconds, at most 15 automatic polls per
        ID, and 600 seconds maximum local retention measured from submission.
        Slots are reserved before policy/admission callbacks, so reentry cannot
        steal an unpublished record. Full reports and submitted source/target
        references are not cached. Stored caller/initiator references are
        authorization metadata. Only terminal, unlocked slots may be evicted
        to admit a new ID; active slots are not evicted for capacity.

        Accepted, pending, completed, failed, and unknown-expired messages
        include the ID. Completion includes updated/current counts and blueprint
        participation; failure includes the primary code. Exhausting the poll
        allowance announces that automatic polling stopped while the outcome
        remains unconfirmed. result(id) can still observe later completion.
        Scheduling failure after admission returns the valid ID with a manual
        polling notice. A subsequent authorized result read can schedule
        housekeeping again. Neither event cancels or fails the driver request.

        On a successful read, result() returns the driver's fresh report.
        If local metadata is absent/expired, or the driver returns its specific
        unknown/expired-ID error, it instead returns a small mapping with id,
        status "unknown-expired", and message. Other driver read errors return
        id, status "report-unavailable", and message while retaining the local
        ID for a later read. Authorization or admission errors raise. These
        wrapper observations are not additional driver request states.

        Local terminal eviction or the 600-second local lifetime can erase
        authorization metadata before the driver report expires. Such an ID
        is no longer accessible through this coordinator even if the master
        can still read it. Local expiry also does not cancel pending work.
        Reads do not extend local retention. Destroying the coordinator has
        the driver's owner-destruction consequences described above; use a
        long-lived coordinator rather than a disposable command object.

OPERATOR WORKFLOW
        After deploying a locally adapted coordinator and master policy:
          id = coordinator.submit("/npc/guard");
          report = coordinator.result(id);

        Keep the returned ID. Pending means wait for a later backend turn.
        Inspect failed reports by exact instance/declaration fields, remove
        the reported incompatible dependency or correct the source, then
        submit a new request. Inspect incomplete markers before treating a
        failure report as complete evidence. For an explicit subset use an
        object array; use ({}) deliberately when only a path-source blueprint
        should change. Use a loaded object source when its existing program
        is the intended candidate. Do not substitute clones() historical
        selections for all-mode execution discovery without intending that
        narrower selection.

        Verify retained values and actual cohort counts after completion.
        There is no constructor/reset replay and no automatic live-mudlib
        deployment, source-control checkout, or remote publication in this
        coordinator. The test/t-blueprint-workflow fixture compiles the shipped
        coordinator and exercises its driver ownership, policy, bounded polling,
        source modes, state preservation, report copying, and diagnostics.

